# Bishop or knight in the ending?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | calm, warm chess coach","b":"gemini:Puck | eager club player, relaxed and good-humoured; never exaggerated"}
prompt: "When is a bishop better than a knight, and when the other way round? How do you trade down into an ending you know is won?"
next: {"title":"How do you win a won game?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/chess-30-days/26-how-do-you-win-a-won-game.cast"}

## Warm-up: king first, then the rook
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: 4k3/8/8/3P1K2/8/8/8/8 w - - 0 1, moves: [d6, Kd7]}
      - {fen: 4k3/8/8/3P1K2/8/8/8/8 w - - 0 1, moves: [Ke6]}
      - {fen: R7/P4k2/8/8/8/8/r7/6K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Rh8, Rxa7, Rh7+, Kg6, Rxa7]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title Warm-up

A: Today: bishop or knight in the ending, and how to trade into an ending you know is won. First, two warm-ups from the last two days.
    draw position

B: My pawn's free. I'll push it: d6.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: Then his king steps in front of it, and it's a draw.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

A: Day twenty-three: the king goes first.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win the game." widget chess line true hints ["The king leads, the pawn follows.","Which square in front of the pawn can your king reach?"] themes ["key squares","opposition"] voice a
        right: King to e6: your king stands in front of the pawn, on a key square, and Black's king can no longer stop the pawn.
        wrong: Bring the king up first, in front of the pawn.

B: King first, then the pawn.
    highlight piece_e6

A: Second, a rook ending. White's pawn is on a7, with the rook in front of it.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: And his rook sits behind my pawn. I can't push, and my rook can't leave.
    highlight piece_a2

A: Yesterday's rule says the rook belongs behind the pawn. Yours isn't, so find another way.
    highlight piece_a8

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win the game." widget chess line true hints ["Move the rook off the a-file, to a square with a future check.","If his rook takes the pawn, which check lines up king and rook?"] themes ["rook ending","skewer"] voice a
        right: Rook to h8. His rook takes the pawn, rook to h7 is check, and when the king steps away, the rook on a7 falls. If he leaves the pawn, it queens and costs him his rook.
        wrong: Your rook can leave the a-file. Where would a check line up his king and his rook?

B: A skewer, in a rook ending.
    highlight piece_a7

A: With his king on g7 instead, it's a draw. Now the minor pieces: bishop against knight.
    point at.ref sq_g7 gesture circle

## Bishop, knight, and the right trade
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: 8/1B2n3/8/P3k3/8/7p/4K3/8 w - - 0 1, moves: [a6, Nf5, a7], arrows: [{from: b7, to: h1}], highlights: [c6, c8]}
      - {fen: 8/1N2n3/8/P3k3/8/7p/4K3/8 w - - 0 1, moves: [a6, Nc6]}
      - {fen: 8/3b1k2/p3p1p1/PpKp3p/1P1P1N1P/6P1/8/8 w - - 0 1, moves: [Kd6, Bc8, Kc6, Ke7, Nxg6+], highlights: [a6, b5, d5, e6, g6, h5]}
      - {fen: 8/6k1/5n2/3N4/5K1P/3B4/8/8 w - - 0 1, moves: [Nxf6, Kxf6, h5, Kg7, Kg5, Kh8], highlights: [h8], arrows: [{from: d3, to: h7}]}
      - {fen: 8/6k1/5n2/3N4/5K1P/3B4/8/8 w - - 0 1, moves: [Ne7]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
sources:
    - id: bishop
      title: Bishop (chess)
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: A bishop can influence both wings at once, a knight less so; a bishop hemmed in by its own pawns is called a bad bishop; on a crowded, blocked board the knight, which jumps, can be the better piece.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bishop_(chess)
    - id: wrongbishop
      title: Wrong bishop
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: King, bishop and rook pawn against a king is a draw when the bishop does not control the pawn's promotion square and the defending king reaches the corner.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wrong_bishop
    - id: tb
      title: Lichess Syzygy tablebase
      authors: Lichess
      year: 2026
      finding: 'Exact results for every position here with seven men or fewer: the bishop position wins only with a6 and draws with a knight on b7; after the knight trade the bishop-and-h-pawn ending is a draw, while Ne7 keeps a win.'
      url: https://tablebase.lichess.ovh

    card title "Bishop or knight?"

A: Bishop or knight: which is better in an ending? Here, White's bishop against Black's knight, a passed pawn each.
    draw position

B: The knight. It jumps, it forks, and a bishop only ever sees one colour.
    highlight piece_e7

A: Look where the bishop stands: on the long diagonal, guarding h1. Black's pawn can't queen there.
    draw arrow_0

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win the game." widget chess line true hints ["Push your passed pawn.","Which squares does the knight need to stop it?"] themes ["minor piece ending"] voice a
        right: Pawn to a6, the knight comes round, and the pawn goes on to a7. The knight's quick routes to the pawn, c6 and c8, are both covered by the bishop: the pawn walks in.
        wrong: Your bishop already holds the h-pawn. What can your own pawn do?

B: One bishop, guarding h1 on one side and c8 on the other.
    draw hl_c6 hl_c8 parallel true

    erase arrow_0 hl_c6 hl_c8

A: Now give White a knight on b7 instead. Same pawns, same kings.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: Pawn to a6, as before.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: Now his knight stops it on c6, and yours is too far from h1: a draw.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

A: A knight only reaches the squares near it. A bishop works from across the board, on both wings at once.
    highlight piece_b7

A: So with an open board and pawns on both wings, the bishop is usually the better piece.
    highlight piece_a6 piece_h3

B: So bishops are just better?
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: Not here. Every Black pawn stands on a light square, the colour of his own bishop.
    draw hl_a6 hl_b5 hl_d5 hl_e6 hl_g6 hl_h5 parallel true

A: They block it, and White's pawns sit on dark squares it can never attack. That's a bad bishop.
    highlight piece_a5 piece_b4 piece_d4 piece_g3 piece_h4

A: Black is even a pawn up, and still worse.
    highlight piece_h5

A: White's king walks in on the dark squares: king to d6 hits the bishop.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: The bishop steps back.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

A: King to c6, and the bishop has no safe square left.
    animate plies_shown 3 duration 1.3

A: Black's king hurries over to guard it,
    animate plies_shown 4 duration 1.3

A: and the knight takes g6, with check. Here, the knight is the better piece.
    animate plies_shown 5 duration 1.3

    erase hl_a6 hl_b5 hl_d5 hl_e6 hl_g6 hl_h5

A: Second question: which trades are safe? Here White is a bishop and a pawn up.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: Then I trade the knights, and it's easy.
    highlight piece_d5 piece_f6

A: Knight takes f6.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: King takes back.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

    quiz "Bishop and pawn against a lone king: win or draw?" on_canvas true buttons_at.x 930 buttons_at.y 400 buttons_layout column voice a
        right: A draw: Black's king gets to h8, and a light bishop can never chase it out of a dark corner.
        wrong: Look at the corner in front of the pawn, and the colour of your bishop.
        * Win
        + Draw

A: Whatever you do, his king runs for h8.
    animate plies_shown 6 duration 4 easing linear

A: Your bishop's diagonal reaches h7, never h8. His king just shuttles in the corner: a draw.
    draw arrow_0 hl_h8 parallel true

B: A whole bishop up, and a draw?
    highlight piece_d3

    erase arrow_0 hl_h8

    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: Keep the knights instead: step yours out of reach, say to e7, and White wins.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: So before a trade, picture the ending after it, and ask: do I know it's won?
    highlight piece_e7 piece_d3

## Your turn: four endings and a game
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: 8/K7/6k1/4P3/8/7p/8/4B3 w - - 0 1, moves: [Bg3]}
      - {fen: 8/6k1/3b4/8/3NP3/4K3/8/8 w - - 0 1, moves: [Nf5+, Kf6, Nxd6]}
      - {fen: 3K4/8/8/1NP5/3n4/4k3/8/8 w - - 0 1, moves: [Nxd4, Kxd4, c6]}
      - {fen: 8/6P1/4K2k/5n2/8/8/1B6/8 b - - 0 1, moves: [Nxg7+, Bxg7], flip: true}
      - {fen: 8/1n6/4k3/7P/1P6/3K4/3B4/8 w - - 0 1}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
sources:
    - id: tb
      title: Lichess Syzygy tablebase
      authors: Lichess
      year: 2026
      finding: 'Exact results: Bg3, Nf5+ and Nxd4 are the only winning moves in their positions, Nxg7+ the only drawing move in the defence, and the bishop-against-knight ending at the end is won after any first move.'
      url: https://tablebase.lichess.ovh

    card title "Your turn"

A: Now you've seen both pieces at work: your turn. Four endings, then a game against the computer.
    draw position

A: First one: Black's king is coming for your pawn, and his h-pawn is running.
    highlight piece_g6 piece_h3

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win the game." widget chess line true hints ["One bishop move can do two jobs.","Which square guards both h2 and your pawn?"] themes ["minor piece ending"] voice a
        right: Bishop to g3: from one square it stops the h-pawn on h2 and guards your pawn on e5.
        wrong: Your bishop has two jobs: the h-pawn and your own pawn. Find one square for both.

A: Next, the knight's turn.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: Pawn to e5 attacks the bishop.
    highlight piece_e4

A: And the bishop just takes it: nothing guards e5.
    highlight piece_d6

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win the game." widget chess line true hints ["Look at the checks first.","A knight check that hits the bishop too."] themes ["fork","minor piece ending"] voice a
        right: Knight to f5, check, forking king and bishop. The king steps away, the knight takes the bishop, and knight and pawn beat a lone king.
        wrong: Which knight check also attacks the bishop?

A: Next. Black's knight attacks yours, and guards c6.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: I'll save my knight.
    highlight piece_b5

A: Then his knight keeps guarding c6, and your pawn is stuck: a draw.
    highlight piece_d4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win the game." widget chess line true hints ["Picture the ending after a trade.","Without his knight, can his king catch your pawn?"] themes ["conversion","square rule"] voice a
        right: Knight takes d4, the king takes back, and the pawn runs: his king can't catch it, and yours guards c7 and c8.
        wrong: Saving the knight lets his knight stop the pawn. What if there were no knights at all?

A: Now you defend, from Black's side of the board. White's pawn is one step from queening.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Save the game." widget chess line true hints ["Can anything of yours take the pawn?","Even with check."] themes ["defence","minor piece ending"] voice a
        right: Knight takes g7, check. The bishop takes back, and with no pawns left, king and bishop can never mate: a draw.
        wrong: A knight is worth less than a new queen. What can it take?

A: Back to White. Bishop against knight, and you're two pawns up,
    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: one on each wing.
    highlight piece_b4 piece_h5

A: Make a new queen within twenty-five moves.
    explore activity vs_computer goal.kind promote goal.within 25 store q25

    hide position

A: So: on an open board with pawns on both wings, the bishop is usually stronger.
    text t_open "Open: bishop" x 500 y 520 font_size 60

A: Pawns stuck on its colour hand the game to the knight.
    text t_closed "Blocked: knight" x 500 y 430 font_size 60

A: And before you trade, know the ending you're trading into.
    text t_know "Know the ending" x 500 y 340 font_size 60

A: Homework: one slow game today, fifteen plus ten or slower. Then find its first mistake.
    text hw "15 + 10" x 500 y 230 font_size 48

## Where next
end_page: true

Next: How do you win a won game?
